16699219, -0.0016927719116210938, 0.006145477294921875, -0.0242767333984375, 0.032989501953125, -0.0225067138671875, -0.00039386749267578125, -0.0299224853515625, -0.0321044921875, -0.0279541015625, -0.025634765625, 0.03558349609375, 0.026824951171875, 0.0179443359375, 0.0107269287109375, 0.0178680419921875, 0.0006060600280761719, 0.004222869873046875, -0.051483154296875, 0.0087890625, -0.0001766681671142578, 0.0037059783935546875, 0.029449462890625, -0.0303497314453125, -0.0035800933837890625, -0.017120361328125, -0.01605224609375, 0.0018281936645507812, 0.017669677734375, 0.006557464599609375, 0.0247344970703125, -0.00144195556640625, 0.01136016845703125, -0.0037097930908203125, 0.018646240234375, -0.01294708251953125, 0.0085296630859375, -0.01397705078125, 0.002704620361328125, 0.01204681396484375, -0.02288818359375, 0.0023345947265625, -0.01262664794921875, -0.0322265625, -0.016632080078125, -0.01409149169921875, 0.0142822265625, -0.034698486328125, 0.007656097412109375, 0.0005202293395996094, 0.020904541015625, -0.0033664703369140625, -0.00589752197265625, -0.0171966552734375, 0.0179595947265625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. In level-k models, \u201clevel-0 actions\u201d refer to the choices made by players who do not engage in any strategic reasoning about others\u2019 moves, while \u201clevel-0 beliefs\u201d are their stated expectations about the choices of other similarly non-strategic players. For example, in a number-guessing game, a level-0 player might simply pick a number at random or choose a salient number, and expect others to do likewise.\n\nThis study uses the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014) to provide new evidence on the nature and perception of level-0 reasoning. We replicate this method\u2014which identifies level-0 reasoning via explicit communication between team members, allowing fine-grained observation of their reasoning process\u2014not only in the established beauty contest game but also in a previously unstudied environment: a variant of the 11-20 game. Our main contributions are (i) extending intra-team elicitation to a new class of strategic games and (ii) demonstrating that observed level-0 behaviors and beliefs are highly sensitive to game structure, thereby challenging the conventional assumption of a fixed, game-independent level-0 type.\n\nWe focus on two classic experimental games. The beauty contest game asks each player to select a number from 0 to 100, with the winner being the player closest to two-thirds of the average of all choices. This game is often used to study iterative reasoning and is standard in level-k experimental literature. The variant of the 11-20 game we examine, adapted from Goeree et al. (2018), requires players to choose an integer between 11 and 20. The payoff structure is such that every player receives a base payoff equal to their chosen number, but any player (or players) who uniquely picks the lowest number earns a substantial bonus; if two or more choose the lowest number, no one gets the bonus. Consequently, \u201cfirst-mover\u201d play (i.e., picking the absolute lowest number without regard to others\u2019 choices) is risky rather than optimal for a non-strategic agent, due to the potential for coordination failure\u2014a dynamic that standard level-k reasoning fails to fully capture.\n\nOur results show clear, context-dependent patterns. In the beauty contest game, as in Burchardi and Penczynski (2014), elicited level-0 beliefs and actions are closely aligned: the majority of teams classified as level-0 both choose and expect others to choose around 50 (mean level-0 action: 51.2, SD: 7.1, N = 14 teams), consistent with the standard assumption of \u201cnaive\u201d or uniformly random play. In the 11-20 game variant, however, level-0 teams display more varied behavior. Most level-0 teams choose and expect numbers near the upper end of the range (mean level-0 action: 18.7, SD: 1.6, N = 11 teams), but there is notable dispersion: some teams cluster at 20, interpreting it as a safe, salient endpoint, while others avoid the absolute minimum (11) entirely. In communication transcripts, these teams explicitly reference \u201cavoiding conflict\u201d or picking a \u201csafe high number,\u201d showing a mix of risk aversion (selecting 20 to guarantee the sure payoff) and focal point reasoning (favoring endpoints as obvious choices).\n\nThese results support and extend the findings of Goeree et al. (2018): patterns commonly attributed to failures of iterative reasoning can also reflect flexible, game-sensitive anchors used by putatively non-strategic players. Our findings thus indicate that both level-0 beliefs and actions are strongly shaped by payoff structure and game context, undermining the idea of a uniform level-0 type. Nevertheless, we acknowledge that intra-team communication may introduce its own limitations\u2014for instance, group discussion could dampen idiosyncratic choices, and classifying teams as \u201clevel-0\u201d based on their justifications may risk underestimating implicit strategic thinking.\n\nIn summary, our evidence suggests that the mapping between level-0 beliefs and level-0 actions is not universal, but game-dependent. This points to the need for revising standard modeling approaches to allow for adaptable level-0 behavioral rules that flexibly account for payoffs and focal points in different settings, rather than assuming random or naive play across environments. Future work should calibrate level-k frameworks to context-sensitive benchmarks and further investigate how intra-team elicitation interfaces with individual cognitive processes."}}